The algorithms DERNA2 implements are described in the published literature,
and the implementation here is the authors' own. The references are in
README.md.

This distribution carries the following third-party data.

  packages/rna-mfe/data/turner2004.params

    The default RNA free-energy parameters: the Turner 2004 nearest-neighbor
    parameters from NNDB, the Nearest Neighbor Database
    (https://rna.urmc.rochester.edu/NNDB/), converted to this project's
    format. Please cite:

      Mittal, A., Turner, D. H. & Mathews, D. H. (2024). NNDB: An expanded
      database of nearest neighbor parameters for predicting stability of
      nucleic acid secondary structures. Journal of Molecular Biology,
      168549. doi:10.1016/j.jmb.2024.168549

      Turner, D. H. & Mathews, D. H. (2010). NNDB: the nearest neighbor
      parameter database for predicting stability of nucleic acid secondary
      structure. Nucleic Acids Research, 38, D280-D282.

      Mathews, D. H., Disney, M. D., Childs, J. L., Schroeder, S. J.,
      Zuker, M. & Turner, D. H. (2004). Incorporating chemical modification
      constraints into a dynamic programming algorithm for prediction of
      RNA secondary structure. Proceedings of the National Academy of
      Sciences USA, 101, 7287-7292.

  packages/derna-codon-dp/data/codon_usage_human.csv
  packages/derna-codon-dp/data/codon_usage_mouse.csv
  packages/derna-codon-dp/data/codon_usage_cho.csv
  packages/derna-codon-dp/data/codon_usage_ecoli.csv
  packages/derna-codon-dp/data/codon_usage_yeast.csv

    The codon usage tables for Homo sapiens, Mus musculus, Cricetulus
    griseus, Escherichia coli and Saccharomyces cerevisiae, from the Codon
    Usage Database at the Kazusa DNA Research Institute
    (https://www.kazusa.or.jp/codon/), maintained by Yasukazu Nakamura.
    Please cite:

      Nakamura, Y., Gojobori, T. & Ikemura, T. (2000). Codon usage
      tabulated from international DNA sequence databases: status for the
      year 2000. Nucleic Acids Research, 28, 292.

    PROVENANCE.md beside those files records which database entry each
    table came from, the corpus it was tabulated over, and its snapshot.

  Protein sequences in the test data (tests/data/*.fasta)

    UniProtKB entries P15421 (GLPE_HUMAN, glycophorin-E) and P0DTC2 (spike
    glycoprotein of SARS-CoV-2), from UniProt (https://www.uniprot.org/),
    used under the Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International licence
    (https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/). Some files hold only
    the first residues of an entry, as their headers say.
